.TH HSH 1 "August 2026" "ALX / Holberton School" "Simple Shell Manual"
.SH NAME
hsh \- simple UNIX command interpreter
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ./hsh
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hsh
is a simple UNIX command language interpreter that executes commands read from standard input or from a script file. It operates in both interactive and non-interactive modes.

When executed without arguments, 
.B hsh
prompts the user with \fB($)\fR and waits for commands. When input is piped or redirected into standard input, it operates silently without displaying a prompt.
.SH INVOCATION
.TP
.B Interactive Mode:
Run the shell directly in a terminal:
.RS
.B $ ./hsh
.br
\fB($)\fR /bin/ls
.br
\fB($)\fR exit
.RE
.TP
.B Non-interactive Mode:
Pipe commands into the executable or redirect from a file:
.RS
.B $ echo "/bin/ls" | ./hsh
.br
.B $ ./hsh < command_file
.RE
.SH COMMAND EXECUTION
The shell reads an entire line using
.BR getline (3),
tokenizes the input based on whitespace delimiters (spaces, tabs, newlines) using
.BR strtok (3),
and executes commands as follows:
.IP 1. 4
If the command contains a slash (\fB/\fR), it is executed directly via
.BR execve (2).
.IP 2. 4
If no slash is found, the shell searches the directories listed in the \fBPATH\fR environment variable for an executable file matching the command name.
.IP 3. 4
A child process is created using
.BR fork (2)
to execute external commands, while the parent process suspends execution using
.BR wait (2)
or
.BR waitpid (2)
until the child process terminates.
.SH BUILT-IN COMMANDS
.B hsh
implements the following built-in commands:
.TP
.B exit [status]
Terminates the shell process. If a numeric \fIstatus\fR is provided, the shell exits with that integer code; otherwise, it exits with the status of the last executed command.
.TP
.B env
Prints the current environment variables to standard output.
.TP
.B setenv [VARIABLE] [VALUE]
Initializes a new environment variable or modifies an existing one.
.TP
.B unsetenv [VARIABLE]
Removes an environment variable from the environment.
.TP
.B cd [DIRECTORY]
Changes the current working directory of the process. If no argument is given, it defaults to the user's \fBHOME\fR directory. Using \fB-\fR switches to the previous directory (\fBOLDPWD\fR).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.B hsh
inherits and manages an array of environment variable pointers (environ). Key variables include:
.TP
.B PATH
A colon-separated list of directories searched when resolving commands.
.TP
.B HOME
The home directory of the current user.
.TP
.B OLDPWD
The previous working directory.
.SH RETURN VALUE
Upon successful execution of a command, the shell returns the exit status of that command. If a command is not found or cannot be executed, an appropriate error message is printed to \fBstderr\fR and an error code (such as 127 or 126) is returned.
.SH ERRORS
Errors are printed to standard error in the exact format of \fB/bin/sh\fR:
.PP
.RS
\fB./hsh: [line_number]: [command_name]: not found\fR
.RE
